The original upstream archive and all nine downstream patches remain selected.
The aarch64 build enables development of the native SDIO frontend; it does not
establish physical radio operation or replace the existing PCIe controls.

For a selected custom kernel, supply SRC_BASE and KERNBUILDDIR together. The
latter must identify that kernel's completed build directory, including its
generated option headers. Preserve source, generated headers, compiler resources,
actual kernel bytes and package identities in the native qualification record.
The build forwards KERNBUILDDIR to the original module Makefile when provided.

WITH_CTF and DEBUG_FLAGS select the original FreeBSD CTF conversion/merge rules.
MK_SPLIT_KERNEL_DEBUG=no keeps a single module payload. The kmod strip command
removes DWARF after installation while preserving CTF. Package consumers must
verify the installed module's CTF and actual kernel loading, not just compilation.

Upstream does not provide an executable test suite in this archive. Native
checksum/patch/build/stage/package checks and independent module consumers remain
distinct from original tests. Do not report the ports no-op test target as a
passing upstream suite.

Before enabling Pi Wi-Fi in a product image, qualify firmware property access,
power/pin sequencing, ACPI SDIO enumeration, board firmware/NVRAM/CLM selection,
association, traffic, recovery and native SD/Ethernet regression. Preserve the
existing amd64 PCIe path and qualify it when shared build flags change. Complete
source/toolchain/SDK and canonical Component/lifecycle admission remain separate.
